i# Mini Game Hub

			sathwik & Manohar

## Project Overview

A secure, multi-user game hub combining Bash scripting for authentication 
and Python (Pygame) for gameplay. Two players log in, pick a game from a 
menu, play through a graphical interface, and see results on a leaderboard.

## How to Run
bash
bash main.sh


## Project Structure

hub/
├── main.sh           # Entry point & authentication
├── game.py           # Game engine & menu
├── leaderboard.sh    # Leaderboard display
├── games/
│   ├── tictactoe.py
│   ├── othello.py
│   └── connect4.py
├── users.tsv         # Usernames & hashed passwords (auto-generated)
└── history.csv       # Game result history (auto-generated)


## Features

- Two-player login with SHA-256 hashed passwords
- Registration for new users
- Three games with full Pygame GUI:
  - Tic-Tac-Toe (9×9 board, 5-in-a-row to win)
  - Othello / Reversi (8×8 board)
  - Connect Four (7×7 grid)
- Persistent leaderboard with win/loss stats
- Matplotlib charts for player and game statistics

## Dependencies

- Python: `pygame-ce`, `numpy`, `matplotlib`
- Bash: `sha256sum` (built-in on Linux/macOS)

## Design Document

### System Design
A base class `BoardGame` defines shared logic — player names, turn 
switching, a NumPy board array, and an overridable win-check method. 
Each game inherits from it and implements its own rules.

### Implementation Plan

 Week , Goal 

 Week 1 , `main.sh` authentication  
 Week 2 , Tic-Tac-Toe and Connect Four with Pygame GUI 
 Week 3 , Othello, leaderboard, history recording 
 Week 4 , Matplotlib charts, testing, report 

